  <page_number>71</page_number>
  <running_head>De Duellis.</running_head>
  <main_text>
    <l n="1">enim duris subesse cogitur,</l>
    <l n="2">multò magis illis, qui officium</l>
    <l n="3">suum uti par est exequuntur;</l>
    <l n="4">quod agunt dum etiam inno-</l>
    <l n="5">centem, si modò ignorent eum</l>
    <l n="6">talem, secundùm allegata &amp;</l>
    <l n="7">probata condemnant. Ut hic</l>
    <l n="8">cogitandum sit, etiam per fal-</l>
    <l n="9">sos testes, nigrum calculum, id</l>
    <l n="10">est, sententiam mortis salvato-</l>
    <l n="11">rem nostrum tulisse; nec tamen</l>
    <l n="12">semet subduxisse. Idem quod</l>
    <l n="13">de Stephano martyre Novi</l>
    <l n="14">Testamenti, &amp; Nabotho in Ve-</l>
    <l n="15">teri Testamento, dicendum</l>
    <l n="16">est.</l>
    <l n="17" para="true">Quinimò, is qui putat, &amp;</l>
    <l n="18">certò novit adversarium esse</l>
    <l n="19">calumniatorem, ne quidem</l>
    <l n="20">debebit eundem provocantem</l>
    <l n="21">sequi. Sic enim publicum ori-</l>
    <l n="22">retur scandalum, sic multa in-</l>
    <l n="23">commoda subsequerentur. Pr-</l>
    <l n="24">ęterquam quod in partes Dei,</l>
    <l n="25">in partes Magistratuum invo-</l>
    <l n="26">caret: insuper se mortis peri-</l>
    <l n="27">culo</l>
  </main_text>
  <notes>Long s is printed as a double-f form in "subesse", "esse" and "tulisse"; transcribed as plain s. The printed "Pręterquam" keeps its ę. A stray mark after "Novi" and small dots around the text are print or scan specks, not transcribed. A faint watermark at the lower right corner is not printed text and is left out.</notes>
</page>
